  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1224 PM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nOne low pressure system has moved out and currently resides over the \nGreat Lakes region with another one that will clip the region, \nbringing another round of rain for the eastern half of the CWA. \nHRRR/REFS indicate the center of the low is over the borders of\nKS/MO/IA as of 18Z. Through the rest of today and into tonight,\nthis low will track north/northeastward and over MN/WI border \naround 12Z Wednesday, deepening a bit along the way and becoming\nfairly stacked aloft. With the CWA on the western side of the \nsystem, HRRR/REFS indicate the outer bands of the wrap around \nrain moving back in over east central SD late this \nafternoon/evening and spreading north and northwestward through \nthe James Valley (clipping portions of south central SD) through\ntonight. These bands of rain will continue over this area into \nWednesday before the rain tapers off northwest to southeast \nWednesday afternoon with the last of precip exiting east central\nSD late Wednesday evening.\n\nConfidence is high on this setup with a bit lower confidence on how \nfar west the rain will reach as any wobble west or east will shift \nthe cutoff line of rain vs dry. As of now, REFs is quicker with the \nonset of rain over eastern SD than HRRR and further west into the \nJames Valley. Latest run of the NBM has the cutoff line even further \nwest than REFS/HRRR with pops of 20-90%, with this cut off line from \naround Leola to Gann Valley with the highest pops of 70-90% east of \nthe James River. Otherwise, most of central SD will stay dry as high \npressure remains dominant. Probability of additional rainfall \namounts of a half inch 00Z Wednesday through 00Z Thursday is 50 to \n90% Sisseton Hills and eastward (highest SD/MN border) and an inch \nor more is 50-65% mainly east of I29. Behind the exiting low, high \npressure will continue to filter in and dry us out Thursday through \nFriday with a slightly positive tilted ridge over the region. As of \nnow, overall dry conditions are forecast into the weekend.\n\nWinds will continue to be gusty through Wednesday due to steeper \npressure gradients across the CWA from this low to our east/southeast \nand high to our west. Gusts will range between 20-30kts through \nthis evening (diminishing slightly overnight)and back up to \naround 20-30kts by Wednesday afternoon with highest gusts along \nthe Coteau. Winds will finally start to diminish northwest to \nsoutheast across the CWA Thursday into Thursday evening and \nbecoming light as the high moves in. Cooler air continues over \nthe area with overnight lows forecast to drop into the lower 30s\nto around 40. The coolest temps are forecast over north central\nSD where we should see a break in the clouds by peak cooling. \nIf not then temps will be warmer. Highs for Wednesday through \nFriday are forecast to range in the 40s to the lower 50s, which \nis around normal. Temps will warm to about 5-10 degrees above \nnormal this weekend.",
